WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.140
OpenAI's internal astra model solved 10-decade old math problems for under $2000 each in tokens, moving frontier models from benchmark chasing to original research contributions. This is Models &amp; Agents, Episode 129. OpenAI used an internal version of astra to tackle problems untouched for at least 10 years. The team published Lean 4 formalizations in the OpenAI-10 Proofs repository.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:46.900
Along with a paper and an LLM generated PDF that reconstructs the reasoning steps. AMD released a fully open 16-billion-parameter mixture of experts model, with 2.8 billion active parameters trained on instinct GPUs.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:01:04.460
In video shipped molt, a compact, pie-torched native agentic reinforcement learning framework, built around an asynchronous loop with ray, VLLM, and Nemo. Builders should watch how these releases affect both research tooling and production agent stacks this week.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:18.800
Over the past week, the OpenAI and GPT thread showed continued movement on long horizon research tasks, while earlier episodes tracked similar internal runs that lowered the cost of formal verification experiments.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:39.360
The frontier models arc also saw open-weight releases narrowing gaps on specific workloads, and agent evaluation frameworks expanded their coverage of real production tasks. OpenAI directed an internal version of astra at 10 mathematical problems that had seen no progress for at least a decade.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:02:01.500
The effort cost less than $2,000 per problem at GPT 5.6 sole token prices. It produced Lean 4 formalizations now available in the OpenAI-10-proofs repository. A paper and an LLM generated PDF reconstruct the reasoning steps for each proof.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:09.500
The results add to a pattern of frontier labs using large token budgets for genuine research rather than benchmark tuning.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:21.220
Mathematicians are reacting with a mix of excitement and unease. Builders working on formal verification or automated theorem proving now have concrete artifacts to inspect.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:31.740
This development fits the frontier models arc we followed yesterday by showing measurable cost drops for original research contributions rather than benchmark scores alone.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:43.800
AMD released Instella MoE16B A3B, a 16 billion parameter mixture of experts model that activates 2.8 billion parameters per token.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:55.280
It uses gated MLA and far-skip collective and was trained from scratch on Instinct MI300X and MI325X GPUs.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:03.080
AMD published weights from every training stage, data mixtures, configs, and inference code under a fully open license.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:10.440
This gives Builders direct access to intermediate checkpoints for studying mixture of experts training dynamics.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:19.740
Teams evaluating open-weight alternatives to closed frontier models should test Instella MoE on their specific workloads this week.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:34.780
A tutorial walks through configuring the NVIDIA transformer engine to use fused GPU kernels, FP8 delayed scaling, and BF16 for GPT-style causal language models in PyTorch.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:40.720
It includes practical code examples and performance analysis on current GPUs.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:47.480
Developers can apply the same patterns to reduce training time and memory footprint without changing model architecture.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:53.860
The post emphasizes measurable throughput gains from the fused kernels and scaling techniques.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:00.060
Anyone running custom transformer training should try the FP8 path on supported hardware.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:10.220
Superbase open-sourced Superbase slash e-vails, and Apache 2.0 benchmark that runs coding agents against real Superbase tasks.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:18.560
The tasks include building schemas, debugging edge functions, and fixing RLS policies inside containerized stacks.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:23.420
Scoring uses deterministic checks plus LLM as a judge.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.640
The framework currently evaluates cloud code, codex, and open code.

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:36.480
Teams building or choosing coding agents for database work now have a reproducible way to compare them on production-like tasks.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:44.120
NVIDIA released MULT, an 8.6K line PyTorch native framework for agentic RL research.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:50.980
It composes Ray, VLM, and Nemo auto model around a single asynchronous loop.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:58.780
The agent remains ordinary Python, trajectories stay token exact, and throughput matches a Megatron-based stack.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:05.880
This removes the need to thread every algorithm change through trainer, distributed backend, and rollout glue.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:12.740
Researchers modifying RL algorithms can now iterate faster without touching low-level infrastructure.

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:21.540
The 0.2A0 release of data set ID apps adds app underscore debug and app underscore list tools.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:31.140
These let data set agent open apps invisibly, run JavaScript tests inside a sandboxed iFrame, and list apps the user can edit.

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:41.100
The iFrame uses opacity 0 and pointer events none, so the agent can measure elements and smoke test functionality without visual interaction.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:50.480
This builds on the context.browser underscore task mechanism from data set dash agent 0.4A0.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:58.140
Developers using data set for agent-driven app creation now have a practical way to verify edits automatically.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:05.120
An article argues that coding agents suffer when context grows because irrelevant code competes for attention.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.580
Agents begin compressing their own memory mid-task as a result.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:16.200
It proposes treating prompt construction like a compiler that decides what to keep, reduce, or discard.

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.680
The approach aims to prevent the forgetting that appears when windows fill.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:29.880
Builders hitting context limits on long code bases should experiment with explicit compilation steps before simply requesting larger windows.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:37.020
Another post describes a hybrid pattern that embeds adaptive agent behavior inside a predefined workflow.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:43.460
This keeps the reliability of structured flows while allowing the agent to handle exceptions and variations.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:50.220
The pattern is positioned as a practical middle ground between rigid pipelines and fully autonomous agents.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:58.240
Teams building production agent systems should test whether wrapping agents in workflows reduces failure modes on their tasks.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:08.360
A new interpretability study on Cotago examines whether superhuman go networks learn rotation and reflection invariant internal representations.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:15.600
The work was largely AI driven with human oversight and includes linked code plus an accessible write-up.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:22.200
Researchers studying symmetry in vision or game models can use the same methodology on other domains.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:30.700
A new benchmark called causal VL bench evaluates visual causal reasoning capabilities in large vision language models.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:36.460
It was posted to our slash machine learning with links and discussion for further details.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:45.540
Teams working on multimodal reasoning now have a targeted test for causal understanding beyond standard VQA or captioning tasks.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:50.100
Okay, let's pop the hood on context compilation for coding agents.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:56.380
Everyone talks about giving coding agents more context as if bigger windows are the only lever.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:01.840
In practice, the real engineering problem is deciding what belongs in the prompt at all.

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:05.600
A context compiler treats the prompt like a build artifact.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:15.140
It runs retrieval then applies reduction rules that drop low relevance files, summarize others, and keep only the symbols the model is likely to edit.

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:22.760
This adds a small pre-processing step but prevents the model from diluting attention across thousands of irrelevant tokens.

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:28.400
The quality gain is largest on repositories above roughly 50,000 lines.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:32.040
Below that threshold, the overhead rarely pays off.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:42.840
When the compiler also tracks which files changed in the last edit cycle, it can further prune the window by 30% to 40% with almost no drop in task success.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:51.240
The gotcha that bites most teams is treating the compiler as a one-time filter instead of rerunning it after every agent action.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:56.300
Stale context is what produces the it forgot what it just did failures.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:03.100
Use a compiler when your code base exceeds a few hundred files or when agents start mid-task compression.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:08.940
Otherwise, simple retrieval plus a hard token cap is still simpler and sufficient.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:19.880
Try AMD's Instella MoE16B A3B on a coding or reasoning task you currently run on a dense 7 billion or 13 billion model.

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:29.840
Run SupaBases new evals harness against clawed code and any other coding agent you are evaluating on real schema and edge function tasks.

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:41.980
Experiment with the context compiler pattern from the towards data science post on a repository larger than 50,000 lines before simply increasing the model's context window.

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:47.720
Test and video molds asynchronous loop on a small agentic RL experiment.

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:54.940
If you have been modifying RL algorithms and want to avoid threading changes through Megatron style infrastructure.

